{"name":"Sunbeam Yellow","hex":"#f0d39d","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sunbeam","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A ray of sunlight; especially, a demarcated, narrow, and intense (relative to ambient light) ray of sunlight (as revealed by, for example, its diffraction among dust particles).","Any of various lycaenid butterflies of the genus Curetis."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sunbeam"},{"term":"yellow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of sunflower petals and lemons; the color obtained by mixing green and red light, or by subtracting blue from white light; the color evoked by light of wavelength around 580 nm; one of the three primary colors in subtractive color systems.","The middle light in a set of three traffic lights, the lighting of which indicates that drivers should stop short of the intersection if it is safe to do so."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a yellow hue.","Lacking courage."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/yellow"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sunbeam","blocks":[{"text":"A sunbeam, in meteorological optics, is a beam of sunlight that appears to radiate from the position of the Sun. Shining through openings in clouds or between other objects such as mountains and buildings, these beams of particle-scattered sunlight are essentially parallel shafts separated by darker shadowed volumes. Their apparent convergence in the sky is a visual illusion from linear perspective. The same illusion causes the apparent convergence of parallel lines on a long straight road or hallway at a distant vanishing point. The scattering particles that make sunlight visible may be air molecules or particulates."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Crepuscular_Rays_Reno_Nevada_USA.jpg/330px-Crepuscular_Rays_Reno_Nevada_USA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunbeam"},{"title":"Yellow","blocks":[{"text":"Yellow is a color between green and orange on the spectrum of light. It is evoked by light with a dominant wavelength of roughly 575–585 nm. It is a primary color in subtractive color systems, used in painting or color printing. In the RGB color model, used to create colors on television and computer screens, yellow is a secondary color made by combining red and green at equal intensity. Carotenoids give the characteristic yellow color to autumn leaves, corn, canaries, daffodils, and lemons, as well as egg yolks, buttercups, and bananas. They absorb light energy and protect plants from photo damage in some cases. Sunlight has a slight yellowish hue when the Sun is near the horizon, due to atmospheric scattering of shorter wavelengths."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg/330px-Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yellow"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Crepuscular_Rays%2C_India.JPG/500px-Crepuscular_Rays%2C_India.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Crepuscular_Rays%2C_India.JPG/1280px-Crepuscular_Rays%2C_India.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Crepuscular_Rays%2C_India.JPG/500px-Crepuscular_Rays%2C_India.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Daytime sunbeams as seen from the ISS, illustrating their parallel nature","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACrepuscular_Rays%2C_India.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Taipei_crepuscular_rays_%E8%87%BA%E5%8C%97%E9%9B%B2%E9%9A%99%E5%85%89_2018.jpg/500px-Taipei_crepuscular_rays_%E8%87%BA%E5%8C%97%E9%9B%B2%E9%9A%99%E5%85%89_2018.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Taipei_crepuscular_rays_%E8%87%BA%E5%8C%97%E9%9B%B2%E9%9A%99%E5%85%89_2018.jpg/1280px-Taipei_crepuscular_rays_%E8%87%BA%E5%8C%97%E9%9B%B2%E9%9A%99%E5%85%89_2018.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Taipei_crepuscular_rays_%E8%87%BA%E5%8C%97%E9%9B%B2%E9%9A%99%E5%85%89_2018.jpg/500px-Taipei_crepuscular_rays_%E8%87%BA%E5%8C%97%E9%9B%B2%E9%9A%99%E5%85%89_2018.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Crepuscular rays as seen from Taipei, Taiwan (2018)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATaipei_crepuscular_rays_%E8%87%BA%E5%8C%97%E9%9B%B2%E9%9A%99%E5%85%89_2018.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Anticrepuscular_rays_from_above.jpg/500px-Anticrepuscular_rays_from_above.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Anticrepuscular_rays_from_above.jpg/500px-Anticrepuscular_rays_from_above.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"These anticrepuscular rays appear to converge at the antisolar point, as viewed from an aircraft above the clouded ocean.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAnticrepuscular_rays_from_above.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Renaissance_Square_in_Stepanakert.jpg/500px-Renaissance_Square_in_Stepanakert.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Renaissance_Square_in_Stepanakert.jpg/1280px-Renaissance_Square_in_Stepanakert.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.or